; dcc_decoder.loom
;
; Decisions (2026-11-03): one cell of S-9.1 table 2.1 (the maximum of an
; unstretched 0 half-bit) could not be read from the posted PDF's text,
; so no check depends on it; dcc_station.loom sends exactly 100 us, which
; meets every reading of it.
;
; NMRA DCC decoder (the receiving side of the model-railway track signal) on
; IN0, delivering the packets for its address, broadcasts and resets to the
; host through OUTQ[1], and counting the packets it drops.
;
; Written on 2026-11-02, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). Nothing in Loom was built for
; DCC. The program timestamps every edge with the timed edge wait (`WAITE
; RX, ANY, T`, SEMANTICS 6.4), built for PS/2 and the bit-banged receivers,
; and the tick counter (`CSRR NOW`, SEMANTICS 4), and keeps its window
; table, its address, its counters and the packet in data memory (LD/ST,
; SEMANTICS 6.11, built for the EEPROM emulation). What the firmware does
; that no hardware does: the half-bit classification against S-9.1's
; receive windows, the 1-bit symmetry check, the preamble hunt, the bit
; pairing, the byte framing and the error-detection byte. It needs the
; FIFOs (PUSH) and data memory, nothing else: no bit engine, no latch.
;
; Pin: RX = IN0, the track signal through an optocoupler or a comparator,
; logic level. Polarity does not matter (S-9.1 note 3: a decoder cannot know
; which rail is which), so every edge counts and a half-bit is the time
; between two edges.
;
; Windows (S-9.1, the posted draft of 2026-06-16, Table 2.1 and Table 2 of
; section 2.2; figures as tools/protomodels/dcc.py cites them). A decoder
; must accept a 1 half-bit of tr1 = 52 .. 64 us, a 0 half-bit of tr0 = 90 ..
; 10000 us (this covers the stretched 0s of section 2.1), and a 1 whose two
; halves differ by up to tr1d = 6 us. The program accepts each window
; widened by M = 1 us at both ends (2 us for the difference, measured from
; two edges), more than its measurement error (below), so every half the
; standard says must be accepted is, and every half more than 2 us outside
; is refused:
;   a 1 half    51 .. 65 us        102 .. 130 ticks
;   a 0 half    89 .. 10001 us     178 .. 20002 ticks (the top is a timeout)
;   a 1         halves differing by 8 us or less    16 ticks
; Between 65 and 89 us a half-bit is neither. A preamble is 20 or more 1
; halves in a row, 10 complete 1s whatever the phase (S-9.2 section A: a
; decoder must not accept fewer than 10 and must not require more than 12);
; the packet start bit is the first 0 half after it, and its second half
; must be a 0 half too, which fixes the pairing of halves into bits. The
; 1-bit difference is checked from the start bit on (in the preamble the
; pairing is not yet known). The packet end bit's two halves count towards
; the next preamble (S-9.2 note 3).
;
; A packet (S-9.2 section A) is then bytes of 8 bits, MSB first, each
; followed by a separator bit: 0 another byte, 1 the packet end bit. It is
; dropped and counted when a bit is outside the windows (a half of neither
; class, a 1 and a 0 half paired, or a 1's halves too unequal), when it has
; fewer than 3 or more than 6 bytes (format), or when the XOR of all its
; bytes, the error-detection byte included, is not 0 (S-9.2 section B).
; After a drop the program hunts for the next preamble (S-9.2 section C).
;
; Tick (set by the host before RUN): one tick is 0.5 us, TICK = f_clk / 2
; MHz: 50 MHz TICK_INT 25; 48 MHz TICK_INT 24; 8 MHz (the RTL tests)
; TICK_INT 4. A deadline must lie less than 2^15 ticks ahead (SEMANTICS 4,
; reached()), which is what keeps the tick from being finer: 10001 us is
; 20002 ticks. Measurement error: each edge is seen 2 to 5 clocks after it
; reaches the pad (synchroniser and slot grid) and NOW is read a fixed slot
; later, so a half-bit is measured to within 3 clocks plus one tick: 0.56 us
; at 50 MHz, 0.875 us at 8 MHz. It stays under M = 1 us from 6 MHz up.
; Processing: WAITE sees an edge only if it is waiting when the edge comes
; (PREV_PINS follows the pin in every slot, SEMANTICS 6.4), so the work
; after one edge must be done before the next. The longest path from a
; WAITE that saw an edge to the next WAITE, counted by hand from the
; listing (LD and ST two slots), is 80 slots: the report, made after the
; edge that follows the packet end bit (`report`). After the end bit's last
; edge it is 52 slots, inside a packet 43. With the edge seen up to 5
; clocks late, 80 slots fit in the shortest half accepted, 51 us, from
; 6.4 MHz up (325 clocks). The program takes the clock from 8 MHz up.
;
; Size: 177 words, 0x080 .. 0x130: it spills 49 words into thread 2's
; quarter, so thread 2 must not be started with this image (the listing
; says so). dcc_station (thread 0, 0x000 .. 0x068) fits beside it.
;
; Host interface (thread 1). Before RUN the host writes the decoder's
; address into the data word `addr` (an IMEM write; the first byte of a
; packet is compared with it, so 1 .. 127 is a baseline address and 192 ..
; 231 the first byte of a long address, RP-9.2.1). Packets whose first byte
; is 0 (broadcasts and the reset packet, S-9.2 section B) are delivered
; whatever the address. Results (FIFO space read, thread 1), per packet:
;   status word: bit 15 = 1, bits 11:8 n, the bytes without the error byte
;       (2 .. 5), bits 7:0 the first byte; then
;   ceil((n - 1) / 2) data words, the bytes after the first, the earlier in
;       bits 15:8 (bits 7:0 are 0 in the last word when n is even).
; The status word is pushed only if OUTQ has room for it (a non-blocking
; test); with OUTQ full the packet is counted as lost. Its data words, at
; most two, are pushed blocking: a host that keeps a report's worth of room
; (empties OUTQ once a packet, about 5 ms) never stalls them. A stall long
; enough to miss an edge loses that packet's successor at worst: the next
; preamble resynchronises the program.
; Counters (data words, read by the host with the threads halted; 16 bits,
; wrapping): `cok` packets received whole (any address), `cxor` dropped for
; the error byte, `cbit` dropped for a bit outside the windows, `cfmt`
; dropped for their length, `clost` whole packets not reported for a full
; OUTQ. Idle packets are received whole and counted in `cok`, never reported.
;
; Registers: r0 the time of the last edge (NOW), r1 the class of a half or
; a bit, and scratch; r2 the length of a half; r3 the first half of a bit,
; and scratch; r4 the byte being received; r5 halves or bits left; r6 the
; XOR of the bytes; r7 the address of the data table `tab`.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 1
        .pins   RX = IN0
        .tick   4                       ; 0.5 us at 8 MHz, the slowest clock (header)
        .equ    K1LO  = 0               ; table offsets: shortest 1 half, ticks
        .equ    K1HI  = 1               ;   longest 1 half
        .equ    K0LO  = 2               ;   shortest 0 half
        .equ    KTMO  = 3               ;   longest 0 half: the timeout
        .equ    ADDR  = 4               ; the decoder's address (host-set)
        .equ    NB    = 5               ; bytes received in this packet
        .equ    COK   = 6               ; counters
        .equ    CXOR  = 7
        .equ    CBIT  = 8
        .equ    CFMT  = 9
        .equ    CLOST = 10
        .equ    BUF   = 11              ; the packet, a byte a word, 6 words
        .equ    DMAX  = 16              ; the largest 1-bit half difference, ticks
        .equ    PRE   = 20              ; 1 halves for a preamble
        .equ    NBMAX = 6               ; bytes in a packet, the error byte included

start:  MOV16   r7, tab
        CSRR    r0, NOW
        LD      r1, r7, KTMO
        ADD     r1, r1, r0
        CSRW    TD, r1                  ; the first timeout

; ------------------------------------------------------------ the preamble
; r5 counts the 1 halves in a row (it saturates at 63).
hunt:   LDI     r5, 0
hloop:  CALL    half                    ; r1: 0 a 0 half, 1 a 1 half, 2 neither
        CMPI    r1, 1                   ; Z = a 1 half, C = (r1 >= 1)
        BZ      h1
        BC      hunt                    ; neither: start again
        CMPI    r5, PRE                 ; a 0 half: after a preamble?
        BNC     hunt                    ; no
        CALL    half                    ; the start bit's second half
        TEST    r1, r1
        BNZ     hunt                    ; not a 0 half: no start bit
        JMP     packet
h1:     CMPI    r5, 63
        BC      hloop
        ADDI    r5, 1
        JMP     hloop

; ------------------------------------------------------------ the packet
packet: LDI     r6, 0                   ; XOR of the bytes
        LDI     r4, 0
        ST      r4, r7, NB              ; no byte yet
byte:   LDI     r5, 8
        LDI     r4, 0
bitl:   CALL    bit                     ; r1: the bit, or 2 outside the windows
        CMPI    r1, 2
        BZ      ebit
        SHLI    r4, 1
        OR      r4, r4, r1
        DJNZ    r5, bitl
        XOR     r6, r6, r4
        LD      r1, r7, NB
        CMPI    r1, NBMAX               ; C = six bytes already
        BC      efmt
        ADD     r3, r1, r7
        ST      r4, r3, BUF             ; buf[NB] = the byte
        ADDI    r1, 1
        ST      r1, r7, NB
        CALL    bit                     ; the separator
        CMPI    r1, 2
        BZ      ebit
        TEST    r1, r1
        BZ      byte                    ; 0: a data byte follows
        LD      r1, r7, NB              ; 1: the packet end bit
        CMPI    r1, 3
        BNC     eshort                  ; fewer than 3 bytes
        TEST    r6, r6
        BNZ     exor                    ; the error-detection byte is wrong
        LD      r1, r7, COK
        ADDI    r1, 1
        ST      r1, r7, COK
        LD      r3, r7, BUF             ; the first byte
        TEST    r3, r3
        BZ      report                  ; 0: a broadcast or the reset packet
        LD      r1, r7, ADDR
        CMP     r3, r1
        BZ      report                  ; this decoder's address
        LDI     r5, 2                   ; the end bit's halves start a preamble
        JMP     hloop

; The report is made after the next edge, so that the work after the end
; bit's last edge and the work after the next one each fit in a half-bit
; (header, Processing). That edge closes the first half of the next
; preamble: its class is kept in r6 and counted once the report is out.
report: CALL    half
        MOV     r6, r1                  ; the class of the half, for the hunt
        SETD    0                       ; TD = NOW: the WAITB below is a test
        WAITB   OUTQ_NF, T
        BT      elost
        LD      r3, r7, BUF             ; the first byte
        LD      r1, r7, NB
        SUBI    r1, 1                   ; n, the error byte not counted
        SHLI    r1, 8
        OR      r1, r1, r3              ; the first byte
        LDI     r2, 0
        LDIH    r2, 0x80
        OR      r1, r1, r2              ; bit 15: a status word
        .bounded "the WAITB above completed with T = 0, so OUTQ had room in that slot's X cycle; only this thread pushes OUTQ (SEMANTICS 6.7)"
        PUSH    r1
        LD      r5, r7, NB
        SUBI    r5, 2                   ; bytes after the first, 1 .. 4
        MOV     r3, r7
        ADDI    r3, BUF + 1
dw:     LD      r4, r3, 0               ; the earlier byte
        SHLI    r4, 8
        ADDI    r3, 1
        DEC     r5
        BZ      dpush                   ; no later byte
        LD      r1, r3, 0
        OR      r4, r4, r1
        ADDI    r3, 1
        DEC     r5
dpush:  PUSH    r4                      ; blocks only if the host let OUTQ fill
        TEST    r5, r5
        BNZ     dw
        JMP     rearm

; Drops: count, re-arm the timeout (a report's SETD replaced it), hunt.
elost:  LDI     r2, CLOST
        CALL    count
rearm:  LD      r3, r7, KTMO
        ADD     r3, r3, r0
        CSRW    TD, r3                  ; the timeout from the last edge again
        CMPI    r6, 1                   ; the half after the end bit a 1 half?
        BNZ     hunt                    ; no: no preamble has started
        LDI     r5, 3                   ; yes: it and the end bit's two
        JMP     hloop
ebit:   LDI     r2, CBIT
        JMP     edrop
efmt:   LDI     r2, CFMT                ; too long: dropped inside the packet
edrop:  CALL    count
        JMP     hunt
eshort: LDI     r2, CFMT                ; dropped at a good end bit, which
        JMP     eend                    ;   counts towards the next preamble
exor:   LDI     r2, CXOR
eend:   CALL    count
        LDI     r5, 2
        JMP     hloop

; count: the counter at tab + r2 goes up by one. Clobbers r1, r3.
count:  ADD     r3, r7, r2
        LD      r1, r3, 0
        ADDI    r1, 1
        ST      r1, r3, 0
        RET

; ------------------------------------------------------------ one bit
; bit: two halves, the pairing fixed by the start bit. r1 = 1 for two 1
; halves differing by DMAX ticks or less, 0 for two 0 halves, 2 otherwise
; (returned at the first half that settles it). Clobbers r2, r3.
bit:    CALL    half
        CMPI    r1, 1                   ; Z = a 1 half, C = (r1 >= 1)
        BZ      bone
        BC      bret                    ; neither: r1 = 2
        CALL    half                    ; a 0 half: the second must be one too
        TEST    r1, r1
        BZ      bret                    ; r1 = 0: a 0
        JMP     bbad
bone:   MOV     r3, r2                  ; the first half's length
        CALL    half
        CMPI    r1, 1
        BNZ     bbad
        SUB     r3, r3, r2              ; first - second, C = (first >= second)
        BC      bpos
        NEG     r3, r3
bpos:   CMPI    r3, DMAX + 1            ; C = (difference > DMAX)
        BC      bbad
        RET                             ; r1 = 1
bbad:   LDI     r1, 2
bret:   RET

; ------------------------------------------------------------ one half
; half: wait for the next edge or the timeout (TD), and classify the time
; since the last edge: r1 = 1 a 1 half, 0 a 0 half, 2 neither (a timeout
; included); r2 its length in ticks; r0 the new edge's time; TD re-armed
; from it. Nothing else is touched (bit keeps a half in r3).
half:   WAITE   RX, ANY, T              ; an edge, or T = 1 at the timeout
        CSRR    r1, NOW                 ; its time, a fixed slot after it
        SUB     r2, r1, r0              ; the length (mod 2^16)
        MOV     r0, r1
        LD      r1, r7, KTMO
        ADD     r1, r1, r0              ; r0 is this edge
        CSRW    TD, r1                  ; the next timeout, from this edge
        BT      hbad                    ; the line stopped: neither
        LD      r1, r7, K1LO
        CMP     r2, r1                  ; C = (length >= shortest 1)
        BNC     hbad                    ; too short for anything
        LD      r1, r7, K1HI
        CMP     r1, r2                  ; C = (longest 1 >= length)
        BC      hone
        LD      r1, r7, K0LO
        CMP     r2, r1                  ; C = (length >= shortest 0)
        BNC     hbad                    ; between the windows
        LDI     r1, 0
        RET
hone:   LDI     r1, 1
        RET
hbad:   LDI     r1, 2
        RET

; ------------------------------------------------------------ data
; The window table in 0.5 us ticks (header), then the host's and the
; program's words. The host writes `addr`; it reads the counters halted.
tab:    .word   102                     ; K1LO  51 us   (tr1 min 52 - M)
        .word   130                     ; K1HI  65 us   (tr1 max 64 + M)
        .word   178                     ; K0LO  89 us   (tr0 min 90 - M)
        .word   20002                   ; KTMO  10001 us (tr0 max 10000 + M)
addr:   .word   3                       ; the address; 3 is the usual factory one
nb:     .word   0
cok:    .word   0
cxor:   .word   0
cbit:   .word   0
cfmt:   .word   0
clost:  .word   0
buf:    .word   0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0
